The '''Sant Cristòfor–L'Estany Rail Tunnel''' is a railway tunnel on the [[Old Grand Subdivision]] in [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]]. The tunnel carries [[CristoCarril|CristoCarril]] [[Line F (CristoCarril)|Line F]] through the [[Serralada d'Estret]], the low ridge of the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range|Yalabirga range]] that forms the watershed divide between the Shendan and Aethiopian coastal plains. At approximately 8.2 km in length, it is the longest rail tunnel on the CristoCarril network. The western portal is located a short distance east of [[Coll Alt (CristoCarril)|Coll Alt]] station, and the eastern portal emerges on the interior side of the ridge, from which the line descends toward the [[Estany de Panacú]] basin and the terminus at [[L'Estany (CristoCarril)|L'Estany]] station in [[Ciutat del Llac Sud]].

== History ==

The tunnel was constructed as part of the [[Old Grand Subdivision]], a freight railway built in the 1910s to service a copper mine at the mountain pass of Coll Alt. The mine extracted copper ore from deposits in the Serralada d'Estret, and the railway was built to transport ore both down the switchback section to the coastal plain and through the tunnel to the interior. Construction of the tunnel began on 8 January 1913 and was completed on 8 January 1915, a period of two years. The tunnel was bored from both ends simultaneously, with one heading advancing from the western portal near Coll Alt and the other from the eastern portal on the interior side of the ridge.

[[Coll Alt (CristoCarril)|Coll Alt]] station opened on 11 April 1915, three months after the tunnel's completion, as the terminus of the freight line. The tunnel initially served freight traffic only, as the track between the eastern portal and [[L'Estany (CristoCarril)|L'Estany]] on the [[Estany de Panacú]] had not yet been laid. The copper mine's output was transported through the tunnel and down the switchback to the coastal plain for processing and shipment.

When the copper mine was eventually depleted and operations ceased, the Old Grand Subdivision fell into reduced use. The corridor was later repurposed for passenger service as part of the development of the CristoCarril commuter rail network. The remaining track between the tunnel's eastern portal and L'Estany was laid by 1920, and passenger service through the tunnel to L'Estany commenced on 18 June 1920, connecting the interior lowlands to the coast by rail for the first time.

== Design and engineering ==

The tunnel bores through the Serralada d'Estret, which reaches elevations of no more than 250 metres in this region. The western portal, near Coll Alt station, is at approximately 185 metres above sea level, while the eastern portal emerges at approximately 120 metres on the interior side of the ridge. The tunnel follows a gentle descending grade from west to east, accommodating the natural topography of the watershed divide.

The tunnel was constructed with a single bore and a single track. The geology of the Serralada d'Estret consists primarily of weathered sedimentary rock, which allowed for relatively rapid boring compared to the hard igneous rock encountered in major mountain tunnels of the period. Construction proceeded simultaneously from both portals, with the two headings meeting near the midpoint of the ridge.

The tunnel is electrified with 25 kV AC overhead lines as part of the CristoCarril system. Maximum speed through the tunnel is 80 km/h, consistent with the speed restriction on the switchback section of the Old Grand Subdivision.

== Operation ==

The tunnel carries both the express and local service patterns of [[Line F (CristoCarril)|Line F]]. The express service runs non-stop between [[Polígon Sud (CristoCarril)|Polígon Sud]] and L'Estany, passing through the tunnel without stopping at Coll Alt. The local service stops at all stations, including Coll Alt, before entering the tunnel.

Due to the single-track configuration, train movements through the tunnel are controlled by signalling, with trains from opposite directions scheduled to avoid simultaneous entry. The two-car train formations used on Line F, necessitated by the switchback section west of Coll Alt, also operate through the tunnel.

